WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.420
Arnold in Happy Days.

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:02.969
The emperor in Mulan.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.860
And of course, Mr. Miyagi in The Karate Kid.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.330
All of these roles and more were portrayed
by Pat Morita.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.500
Keep watching to find out more about this
esteemed actor's lengthy career and his life

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.530
outside of acting.

00:00:15.173 --> 00:00:17.430
Mr. Miyagi is a master of martial arts.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:22.760
He's such an impressive and affecting mentor,
he managed to reach kids in the real world.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.449
The Karate Kid's popularity is often cited
as a primary force in the rise of youth martial

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:28.449
arts academies.

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:33.980
However, Morita's childhood was spent far
from the world of strip-mall karate classes.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:38.540
He actually suffered from a rare disease and
was told he'd never be able to walk.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.930
He spent almost a decade of his life in a
TB ward called the Weimar Institute.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.440
Eventually, Morita received an experimental
treatment that worked.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.660
Morita defied grim expectations and overcame
the worst-case scenarios for his disease.

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:56.559
During his illness, Morita was often totally
immobilized in a full-body cast that ran from

00:00:56.559 --> 00:00:58.170
neck to thigh.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.440
Because of this, he spent much of his time
staring at the ceiling, trying to come up

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.969
with ways to keep himself entertained.

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:07.270
This is where Morita developed his sense of
humor and comedic timing.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.459
"If it wasn't for my disease, I wouldn't be
here today."

00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:14.920
The hardship of his childhood helped form
his personality and made him who he was later

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:15.920
in life.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.909
When Morita was released from the hospital
with his disease in check, he was reunited

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:21.179
with his family.

00:01:21.179 --> 00:01:27.240
Unfortunately, the year was 1943, and Morita's
family were Japanese immigrants.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.170
He recalled being escorted by an FBI agent
to the Gila River War Relocation Center in

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.259
Arizona.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:37.450
After a little over a year spent there, his
family was transported to California's Tule

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.549
Lake War Relocation Center.

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:43.130
Morita was too young to understand why they
were being held.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.859
Morita's anger grew as he got older and began
to comprehend the truth of the way his family

00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:48.602
was treated.

00:01:49.131 --> 00:01:53.020
After the war ended, Morita's family opened
a restaurant in Sacramento.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.409
Sadly, his father passed away in 1956.

00:01:56.409 --> 00:01:59.619
Morita then took an office job to help support
his family.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:02.779
After years of this work, he decided he needed
to make a change.

00:02:02.779 --> 00:02:07.389
Remarkably, Morita's sense of humor had remained
intact through all this tragedy.

00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:09.810
He started working as a stand-up comedian.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.590
"Those crazy kids.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.799
They eat at home, come here and use the telephone.

00:02:14.799 --> 00:02:17.110
No wonder all the business in the toilet!"

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:21.769
His birth name was Noriyuki Morita, but he
used "Pat Morita" as a stage name because

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:24.569
Pat was such a popular name at the time.

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:29.239
Part of what makes Mr. Miyagi such an effective
character in The Karate Kid is Morita's comedic

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:31.250
timing and subtle humor.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:36.189
Stand-up comedy, which he embarked upon in
the early 1960s, is where he honed that particular

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:37.189
talent.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:39.849
Morita was known for his self-deprecating
sense of humor.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:42.410
He would often make himself the butt of his
own jokes.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.859
It wasn't long before Morita got a better
chance to showcase his talents.

00:02:46.859 --> 00:02:51.300
Morita started landing a few bit parts in
film and television in the mid-1960s.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.250
They were often roles that would be seen as
problematic and stereotypical today.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:03:01.349
One of his first-ever film roles was as "Oriental
#2" in the musical Thoroughly Modern Millie.

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:05.999
Morita quickly started cropping up in sitcoms
like The Cosby Show and Green Acres.

00:03:05.999 --> 00:03:09.060
Two particular roles helped him break more
into the mainstream.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.650
Morita landed the role of Arnold Takahashi
on Happy Days, and he had a recurring role

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.840
as Ah Chew on Sanford and Son.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.349
This mainstream success actually led to a
history-making moment for Morita.

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:25.159
It propelled him into a starring role on Mr.
T and Tina as Taro Takahashi.

00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:29.159
This was one of TV's first predominantly Asian-American
sitcoms.

00:03:29.159 --> 00:03:33.510
Unfortunately, the show only lasted a few
episodes, but it was an important moment in

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.647
in Morita's career and in TV history.

00:03:36.161 --> 00:03:40.530
In hindsight, it's easy to look at a film
like The Karate Kid and identify Pat Morita

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.359
as perfect for the role of Mr. Miyagi.

00:03:43.359 --> 00:03:47.780
He embodies the character so flawlessly that
it's impossible to imagine anyone else playing

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:48.780
him.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:52.709
However, Morita traveled a long road to get
to the role that would make him a legend.

00:03:52.709 --> 00:03:57.930
Jerry Weintraub, the producer behind The Karate
Kid, wouldn't even entertain the idea of Morita

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.639
playing the role.

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:03.290
Weintraub's reasoning was that Morita was
a comedian, and the role of Miyagi was too

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.569
nuanced for a comic to handle.

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:10.010
The director of The Karate Kid, John Avildsen,
held a private audition with Morita, which

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:11.049
he recorded.

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:15.480
During a later meeting with Weintraub, Avildsen
started playing the tape without telling Weintraub

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:16.540
what was going on.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:21.000
The recorded performance convinced Weintraub
that Morita might actually be able to handle

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.000
the role.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.760
Morita still had to audition five different
times before being cast.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.790
"Noriyuki Morita was nominated for an Academy
Award for his performance!

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.656
Ralph Maccio showed up."

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:37.270
Morita's career was taken to new heights with
his role as Miyagi in The Karate Kid and its

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:38.270
sequels.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:39.960
He was even nominated for an Oscar.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.840
Morita continued to appear in guest spots
on a variety of television shows, including

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.039
appearances on The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air,
Murder, She Wrote, and Family Matters.

00:04:48.039 --> 00:04:50.550
He started landing roles as a voice actor
as well.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:55.500
Children of the 1990s may recall Morita as
the host of Britannica's Tales Around the

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.200
World, in addition to his performance as the
Emperor in Disney's Mulan, a role he reprised

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.949
in the sequels and video games like Kingdom
Hearts 2.

00:05:02.949 --> 00:05:09.860
He even had a posthumous guest role as Master
Udon on a 2006 episode of SpongeBob SquarePants.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:14.389
Morita mostly stuck to guest appearances and
film roles after The Karate Kid, but he occasionally

00:05:14.389 --> 00:05:16.319
took recurring roles on television.

00:05:16.319 --> 00:05:20.419
For example, Morita was part of the central
cast of the kids' show The Mystery Files of

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:22.340
Shelby Woo in the late 1990s.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:26.330
He also played Uncle Pat on the Barney-esque
Adventures with Kanga Roddy.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:33.190
After a long and varied career, Morita died
of kidney failure in 2005 at the age of 73.

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:37.370
As fans went on to learn, Morita's lifelong
struggle with alcoholism played a part in

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:38.370
his death.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:42.160
Morita's wife, Evelyn, has said that she was
well aware of her husband's addiction, and

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.180
that she knew it was only a matter of time
before it took him.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.190
The actor himself was aware of it too, and
reportedly told his wife that he knew he couldn't

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:50.530
get over his addiction.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:54.330
Morita's daughter Aly has commented on her
father's addiction as well.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:58.520
Just like Evelyn, she recognizes what a kind
and caring soul her father had, underneath

00:05:58.520 --> 00:05:59.520
it all.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.199
She has also said that her father struggled
with being "a washed-up movie star" following

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:04.860
his Karate Kid success.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.600
Still, as she argues, Morita managed to earn
"a little place in history."

00:06:09.186 --> 00:06:14.330
Morita's legacy lives on, largely due to his
unforgettable performance as Mr. Miyagi.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:18.810
Few who have seen The Karate Kid can forget
Miyagi's lessons to Daniel LaRusso, and the

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.530
film still plays a significant role in pop
culture.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:26.470
The Netflix series Cobra Kai centers around
several prominent characters from the franchise.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.800
It features a scene in which Daniel visits
Miyagi's grave.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:33.440
Had Morita still been alive, there is no doubt
that he would have made a triumphant return

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:34.440
to the series.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.580
Ralph Macchio has said it was important to
him and the rest of the cast and crew to pay

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:39.580
tribute.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.780
"He's woven throughout our show."

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.970
Morita may be gone, but he is a long way from
being forgotten.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:50.150
If you or anyone you know is struggling with
addiction issues, help is available.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:53.909
Visit the Substance Abuse and Mental Health
Services Administration website or contact

00:06:53.909 --> 00:06:56.400
SAMHSA's National Helpline at 1-800-662-HELP.

